                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire. Balliol
                        College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>‘This is not merely a twin to MS
                        208, but, as the list of contents on f. 184v of that volume shows, was
                        originally bound up with it. This combined comment on books i–iii, which I
                        take to be French in origin, became at an early date associated with MS 303,
                        which contains the comment on book iv and is certainly Italian; and the
                        unknown ‘Editor’, in a very skilful Italian hand, using red freely, then
                        made some corrections and additions, foliated the works all through both
                        volumes, and added the folio-references to the lists of quaestiones,
                        providing new lists when required [...] Both volumes had left Italy and come
                        to Oxford before 1376, when MS 303 was pledged there; some time before the
                        late 14th-cent. College inventory was made MSS 302 and 208 had been
                        separately bound, and all three volumes had come to Balliol from some
                        unknown donor. The Balliol inventory-mark 109 liber theologie is at the top
                        of 1; there are notes of valuation: iiij or. marc’ on 2 and iij li. on 3.
                        One would expect a staple-mark, as in MS 208, but old fly-leaves are
                        lacking.’ (Mynors, Balliol, 322–3)</p></provenance>
               </history>
